// examples/sort.tnt
//
// Why isn't there a plain "reversible bubble sort" example? Because there's
// no such thing, and that's the interesting part (this is basically a
// worked illustration of Landauer's principle: sorting is inherently
// information-destroying unless you keep what you threw away).
//
// A comparison sort normally does `if a[i] > a[j] then swap fi <retest>`.
// Once the array is sorted, "did a swap happen here?" is gone -- the
// post-state of a comparator that swapped an out-of-order pair looks
// *identical* to one that found an already-ordered pair and did nothing,
// because both leave the smaller value first. There is no exit assertion
// you can write using only a[i] and a[j] that tells those two histories
// apart, so a naive Tenet transliteration of bubble sort simply doesn't
// type/run -- you cannot pick a testExit that's provably correct for both
// branches, and if you fake one, `uncall` will scramble the array on some
// inputs. Try it: this file's whole point is that you *can't* skip the
// `decisions` stack below and still have something reversible.
//
// The fix is to keep the one bit of information each comparator destroys:
// push a 0/1 "did I swap" flag for every comparator, in order. That stack
// is, bit for bit, the permutation the sort applied. `decisions` ends up
// holding exactly as many bits as there are comparators (10, here) --
// Landauer's bound, made concrete and countable.
//
// This is an odd-even transposition network on 5 elements: n=5 rounds of
// adjacent compare-swaps (alternating "even" pairs (0,1)(2,3) and "odd"
// pairs (1,2)(3,4)) provably sort any 5 elements, so every comparator
// position is fixed in advance -- no data-dependent control flow beyond
// the swap decision itself.

int a[5];
int d;
stack decisions;

proc sort5() {
  // round 1 (even)
  if a[0] > a[1] then a[0] <=> a[1]; d += 1; else skip; fi d == 1; push d decisions;
  if a[2] > a[3] then a[2] <=> a[3]; d += 1; else skip; fi d == 1; push d decisions;
  // round 2 (odd)
  if a[1] > a[2] then a[1] <=> a[2]; d += 1; else skip; fi d == 1; push d decisions;
  if a[3] > a[4] then a[3] <=> a[4]; d += 1; else skip; fi d == 1; push d decisions;
  // round 3 (even)
  if a[0] > a[1] then a[0] <=> a[1]; d += 1; else skip; fi d == 1; push d decisions;
  if a[2] > a[3] then a[2] <=> a[3]; d += 1; else skip; fi d == 1; push d decisions;
  // round 4 (odd)
  if a[1] > a[2] then a[1] <=> a[2]; d += 1; else skip; fi d == 1; push d decisions;
  if a[3] > a[4] then a[3] <=> a[4]; d += 1; else skip; fi d == 1; push d decisions;
  // round 5 (even)
  if a[0] > a[1] then a[0] <=> a[1]; d += 1; else skip; fi d == 1; push d decisions;
  if a[2] > a[3] then a[2] <=> a[3]; d += 1; else skip; fi d == 1; push d decisions;
}
